<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">March of Tuscany</h1><p class="st-meta">846 CE – 1197 CE (approx.) · capital Lucca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great march of Tuscany, ruled from Lucca and later by the house of Canossa, whose Countess Matilda backed the papacy against the emperor.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingians</a> governed <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-tuscany">Tuscany</a> through counts at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a>. In 846 Adalbert I, of the Bonifacian family, succeeded his father and gathered the counties of Tuscany into a march, with the duty of defending the coast against Arab raids. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalbert-ii-of-tuscany">Adalbert II</a>, called the Rich, made the margraves among the most powerful lords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The margraves ruled a vast territory from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a>, with influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and over the Italian crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-tuscany">Hugh the Great</a>, who died in 1001, endowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badia-fiorentina">Badia of Florence</a> founded by his mother Willa. From 1027 the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canossa-castle">Canossa</a> held the march; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Countess Matilda</a> (ruled 1076–1115) hosted the meeting at Canossa in 1077 where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry IV</a> sought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-vii">Pope Gregory VII's</a> forgiveness, and fought for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> for decades.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Matilda died in 1115, her will, leaving her lands to the Church, set pope against emperor. Imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> governed the march while the cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-florence">Florence</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-lucca">Lucca</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-siena">Siena</a> grew into self-governing communes. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Henry VI</a> died in 1197 the Tuscan cities formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-san-genesio">League of San Genesio</a>, ending the march.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/march-of-tuscany.jpg" alt="March of Tuscany" width="320" height="278"><figcaption>Image: Dodolulupepe · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marches_of_the_Kingdom_of_Italy_in_the_900s.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>846 CE – 1197 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lucca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adalbert II, Hugh the Great, Matilda of Canossa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin, Tuscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Margraviate of Tuscany, Tuscia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1022&amp;focus=march-of-tuscany" aria-label="March of Tuscany on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4932 624 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where March of Tuscany lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5252 880L5252 880M5252 880L5218 839M5252 880L5250 884M5252 880L5270 883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5252 880h0M5218 839h0M5250 884h0M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5252 880h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5252 880h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lucca. <a href="/atlas?year=1022&amp;focus=march-of-tuscany">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
